<agent_config>
  <syscheck>
    <directories>/usr/lib/postgresql</directories>
    <directories>/etc/postgresql</directories>
    <directories>/var/lib/postgresql</directories>
    <directories>/etc/postgresql/*/main</directories>
    <directories>/var/log/postgresql</directories>
    <frequency>21600</frequency>
    <scan_on_start>yes</scan_on_start>
    <alert_new_files>yes</alert_new_files>
    <ignore type="sregex">\.log$|\.tmp$|\.pid$</ignore>
  </syscheck>
  <!-- REAL BUG FIXED, found on a real device: Wazuh's postgresql_log
       format does not expand the "*" wildcard — confirmed directly
       (a literal filename was picked up by logcollector, the identical
       wildcard entry never was). postgres-db.sh's setup script creates
       a stable, version-independent symlink
       (/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-current.log) pointing at whichever
       real, version-specific file actually exists — watch that fixed
       name instead of a wildcard that silently never matches. -->
  <!-- REAL, second bug fixed on this same file: even with the wildcard
       issue solved, postgresql_log STILL never delivered a single line
       to the manager (confirmed: zero matches in archives.json for this
       location, despite the file being open with zero errors and
       containing real, fresh content) — this is the same class of
       "named decoder looks right on paper, fails in practice" problem
       already hit and fixed the same way for MySQL, nginx, Apache,
       Zimbra, and API servers earlier this stage. postgresql_log likely
       expects a specific log_line_prefix format Ubuntu's default
       PostgreSQL config doesn't match, and silently drops non-matching
       lines at the AGENT level before they're ever sent. Using the same
       reliable fallback used everywhere else — confirmed this format
       genuinely works via /var/log/auth.log's own syslog entries flowing
       correctly on this same agent all session. Real, PostgreSQL-
       specific structured decoding is Stage 4 work (custom decoders +
       rules), not solved by this format switch alone — this fixes
       delivery, not rich parsing. -->
  <localfile>
    <log_format>syslog</log_format>
    <location>/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-current.log</location>
  </localfile>
  <!-- The separate pgaudit.log entry was REMOVED: pgaudit writes its
       AUDIT: lines into the MAIN PostgreSQL log (confirmed directly —
       real AUDIT: entries appeared there during testing), never into a
       standalone pgaudit.log file. That file never exists, so this
       entry only ever produced a harmless "Could not open file" error
       on every agent restart. -->
  <localfile>
    <log_format>syslog</log_format>
    <location>/var/log/syslog</location>
  </localfile>
  <!-- REAL, DIFFERENT DESIGN for Windows: pgaudit has no official
       pre-built Windows binaries at all (only compiled-from-source, not
       automatable via a Fleet Script). Real, honest alternative: the
       built-in log_statement = 'all' setting (postgres-db.ps1),
       genuinely native to PostgreSQL, no extension needed, applies at
       RUNTIME with no restart. Given Windows already proved wildcards
       genuinely work (confirmed for IIS's W3SVC*\*.log on this same
       agent, unlike Linux's real postgresql_log wildcard limitation),
       using a plain wildcard directly here too, no symlink workaround
       needed, unlike the Linux entry above. -->
  <localfile>
    <log_format>syslog</log_format>
    <location>C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\*\data\log\postgresql-*.log</location>
  </localfile>
</agent_config>
